---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Driver usage"
   summary="The low-level driver layer exists so an agent can operate VoiceOver, NVDA, or the virtual reader the way a person would. It is intentionally plain: start a session, move, read, type, checkpoint, stop."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>What the driver is for</h2>
      <p>
         Use <code>drive</code> when you need raw control. That means next item, previous item, keyboard
         chords, interaction mode, reading spoken output, and grabbing logs. If a named pattern already
         exists, use that instead. Patterns are easier to repeat and much easier to review later.
      </p>
      <p>
         If you are still deciding whether this is the right layer, start with <a href="/workflows#drive-a-session">the driver workflow</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Basic session</h2>
      <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive start --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/landmarks.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive next --session drv_example --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive read --session drv_example --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive stop --session drv_example --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What the raw actions are</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>start</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>stop</code><span>Create, inspect, and tear down a persistent session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>next</code>, <code>previous</code>, <code>interact</code>, <code>stop-interacting</code><span>Move through the target and switch interaction modes.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>key</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>click-current-item</code><span>Send direct commands when you need to act like a real operator.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>read</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>clear-logs</code>, <code>checkpoint</code><span>Capture what the screen reader said and keep the run debuggable.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Persistent versus ephemeral</h2>
      <p>
         Most serious runs should use a persistent session so the transcript, checkpoints, and state stay
         together. The <code>--ephemeral</code> flag is better for one-off actions than for a full review
         pass.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>Important limit</h2>
      <p>
         A transcript is evidence, not a verdict. "The virtual reader said X" is useful. "Therefore the
         page complies with WCAG" is a different statement and often a much bigger one.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
